    slug: 'campaign-finance-101',
    title: 'Campaign Finance 101: Where the Money Comes From',
    date: '2026-01-20',
    author: 'BallotWatch Editorial',
    excerpt: 'The relationship between money and politics is complicated, regulated, and often misunderstood. Here\'s a straightforward look at how campaign funding actually works.',
    tags: ['Campaign Finance', 'Elections'],
    content: [
      'Money in politics is one of the most discussed, and most misunderstood, aspects of the American political system. Everyone has an opinion about it, but relatively few people understand the actual mechanics. How does campaign money flow? Who gives it? What can it buy? And what are the rules?',
      'Start with the basics. Federal candidates raise money primarily through their campaign committees. Individual donors can give up to a set limit per election (it\'s adjusted for inflation every two years, currently a few thousand dollars per election). These direct contributions go straight to the candidate\'s campaign and can be used for ads, staff, travel, and other campaign expenses.',
      'But direct contributions are just one piece of the puzzle. Political parties also raise and spend money through national, state, and local party committees. Party committees can coordinate with candidates to some degree, though the rules about what counts as "coordination" are notoriously complex. Then there are political action committees, PACs, which pool contributions from individuals, corporations, or unions to support candidates or causes.',
      'The biggest change in campaign finance over the past fifteen years has been the rise of super PACs and other outside spending groups. Following the Citizens United decision in 2010, organizations can raise unlimited money to spend on elections as long as they don\'t coordinate directly with campaigns. This created a parallel universe of political spending that dwarfs what campaigns raise on their own.',
      'So where does all this money come from? The Federal Election Commission (FEC) requires campaigns, PACs, and party committees to disclose their donors above certain thresholds. This means you can actually look up who\'s giving money to your representatives. Individual donors span a wide range, from small-dollar grassroots contributors giving twenty bucks to maxed-out donors writing checks at the legal limit.',
      'Industry patterns are revealing. Financial services firms tend to give heavily to members of the banking and finance committees. Defense contractors focus on Armed Services committee members. Healthcare companies target those with jurisdiction over Medicare, Medicaid, and FDA oversight. These patterns don\'t prove corruption, but they reflect a rational strategy: give money to the people who make decisions that affect your industry.',
      'What does campaign money actually buy? Not votes, in most cases, at least not directly. Research on the relationship between donations and votes is mixed. What money does buy is access. Donors who max out their contributions are more likely to get meetings with members, invitations to events, and the chance to make their case directly. In a town where time and attention are the scarcest resources, that access matters.',
      'Small-dollar fundraising has become increasingly important. Online platforms have made it possible for candidates to raise significant sums from millions of small donors, reducing dependence on large contributors. This has shifted the incentive structure somewhat, candidates who can generate grassroots enthusiasm have a genuine alternative to the traditional fundraising circuit.',
      'Transparency is the best tool citizens have for understanding campaign finance. The FEC publishes detailed records of contributions and expenditures. Nonprofit organizations like the Center for Responsive Politics compile this data into searchable databases. You can look up any federal candidate and see exactly who\'s funding their campaign.',
      'The debate over campaign finance reform is ongoing and likely always will be. But regardless of where you stand on the policy questions, understanding how the current system works is essential. Follow the money, not because it tells you everything, but because it tells you something important.'
    ]
